  (E, "Modular arithmetic is met outside cryptography as well, in clocks, in check digits and in the checksums of this chapter's later sections. In Bitcoin it appears whenever a coordinate of a point is calculated, and the prime in question is p = 2 to the 256 minus 2 to the 32 minus 2 to the 9 minus 2 to the 8 minus 2 to the 7 minus 2 to the 6 minus 2 to the 4 minus 1, a very large prime number [BK]. The standard gives the same prime in hexadecimal, as FFFFFFFF followed by repeated groups of F and ending in FFFFFC2F, and in the same long form [SEC2]. Written more briefly it is 2 to the 256 minus 2 to the 32 minus 977, an equality that the chapter's checks verify."),
