  (Y, "The reason for choosing such a curve is that its points can be added to one another by a simple rule, so that the points form a system on which multiplication by a whole number is easy to compute and its inverse is believed hard. Those two properties together give Bitcoin its one-way function. The curve is not valuable for its shape but for the algebra that its points obey."),
  (E, "Over the real numbers the curve is a smooth line, drawn in the book as a figure that is symmetric about the x-axis, which is to say that it is reflected like a mirror by the axis [BK]. Over a finite field it becomes a scatter of dots on a grid. The book shows the same equation over the field with 17 elements, and the chapter's checks find that it has exactly 17 points whose coordinates are whole numbers from 0 to 16, which together with the point at infinity makes 18. Bitcoin's own curve is, in the book's words, a much more complex pattern of dots on an unfathomably large grid [BK]."),
