  (E, "Hash functions occur throughout this book. Chapter 1's proof of work is a search for an input whose hash has a required form, Chapter 3 verified that a downloaded release file has the published hash, and this chapter uses hashes for addresses and for checksums. The program command sha256sum, which the book uses in its example, computes one of them from the command line."),
  (H, "The chapter's checks show the behaviour on the book's own example. The sentence the book hashes, followed by the line break that the echo command adds, has the SHA256 value 94d7a772612c8f2f2ec609d41f5bd3d04a5aa1dfe3582f04af517d396a302e4e, as printed in the book, and the same input gives it every time. Two inputs that differ in a single bit, the letter a and the character just before it in the ASCII table, give outputs that differ in 139 of their 256 bits, a little more than the half that one would expect from an unrelated pair: a tiny change in the input scrambles the output completely, so a near-miss reveals nothing about the right input."),
  (K, "A hash is not an encryption: there is no key and no way to recover the input from the output except by guessing it. For the same reason it is not a good way to hide a small set of possible inputs. If the answer can only be one of a handful of years, anyone can hash all the candidates and compare, so a commitment hides a secret only when the set of possible secrets is huge. Finally, 'secure' is relative to output size: the shorter the output, the easier it is to find collisions, as the discussion of collision attacks later in the chapter shows."),
